# A tiny MakeBelieve manifest. Each rule declares one generated file:
#
#     @/<output> = run <command>
#     @/<output> = capture <command>
#     @/<output> = copy <path>
#
# The command runs through the system shell with its working directory set to
# this folder (so `input.txt` below resolves here). With `run`, `%out` is
# replaced with the path the command should write its result to; with
# `capture`, whatever the command writes to standard output is the result.
# `copy` runs nothing: it names a file in this folder, and its bytes are the
# result. Comments and blank lines are ignored.
#
# The `run` and `capture` commands use `cmake -E`, CMake's built-in
# cross-platform command shim, so the same manifest works whether the shell is
# sh or cmd.exe (cmake just needs to be on PATH). `copy` runs no command at
# all, so it needs nothing on PATH.

@/hello.txt    = run cmake -E echo "hello, makebelieve" > %out
@/greeting.txt = copy input.txt
@/echoed.txt   = capture cmake -E echo "captured from stdout"
